Draw a bottom surface fixed to its output once, where it is

niri draws each background and bottom layer surface once on every
workspace, moved with it, so the two copies of one slide past each
other as workspaces switch. The margin cat sits on the bottom layer,
so as a switch began a second cat rode in sitting on the incoming
workspace's window, before the shell had heard of the switch, and
vanished as the shell's own move began: one to six frames at 120 Hz.

A bottom surface whose namespace NIRI_FIXED_TO_OUTPUT names, among
others split by commas, is now drawn once on its output, where it is,
under every window and over every workspace's own bottom surfaces and
background, and the pointer finds it there too. While the overview is
shown, it goes with each workspace as before, since the workspaces
are zoomed there. Background surfaces are left as they are: each
workspace's background lies over them. The name is read from the
environment, so the config stays one a niri before this reads too.

